WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.260
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:12.240
Today we are going to transform ourselves into&nbsp;
cartoon characters. And it is going to be amazing.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:19.800
But how? Well, for instance, let’s start out&nbsp;
from style transfer. Style transfer means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:27.480
mixing two images together, one for content,&nbsp;
reimagined with the other one for style. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.840
also works for video, what’s more, there are also&nbsp;
computer graphics techniques that can update these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:41.640
virtual worlds in real time as we mark up these&nbsp;
examples on a piece of paper. How cool is that!

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.820
That is all well and good, but that’s nothing&nbsp;
compared to what you are going to see today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.920
because now, we are going to&nbsp;
run style transfer…on ourselves!

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:59.940
That sounds great! But, wait, that’s not&nbsp;
exactly a new idea, previous techniques have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.960
already tried that, so our question is, have&nbsp;
they done it well? Let’s have a look. Oh my,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:13.560
these are not so good. And even the better ones&nbsp;
are not nearly ready for prime time yet. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:20.280
hold on to your papers because we now have a new&nbsp;
technique, where again, in goes a video of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:30.840
and a target style, and we get…this! Whoa.&nbsp;
These are so much better! So cool! And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:37.500
only we could try it ourselves right now…maybe&nbsp;
that is possible, I’ll tell you in a moment.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.620
And it doesn’t stop there, this paper&nbsp;
has a ton more in the tank. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.840
this slider is incredible, by&nbsp;
using it, we can tell the AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.540
how much should the style influence the&nbsp;
video. These results are going by quick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:00.660
so I’ll stop the process here and there so we can&nbsp;
have a little more time to have a look together.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:07.440
I particularly liked the fact that we have a&nbsp;
ton of control over the jawline and the eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.800
and of course, if we wish, these features&nbsp;
can get exaggerated a great deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:21.120
or we can be a little more subtle with them. And&nbsp;
everything in between these two are also possible!

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:27.180
And, have a look at this one too, this&nbsp;
is one of my favorite parts of the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:36.060
Are you seeing it? Well, look here. Our input&nbsp;
person has long hair, but the style reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:43.680
has short hair. And I love how the technique&nbsp;
reimagines the input person’s hair as well in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:51.240
style of the reference. It doesn’t even break a&nbsp;
sweat. This is such an amazing usability feature.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.040
And it supports a variety of&nbsp;
different styles. Just choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.020
the movie and the character of your&nbsp;
liking, and there we go. Loving it.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:07.020
And just think about the fact that a&nbsp;
couple papers before this one, in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:13.680
this was possible. Even inpainting real&nbsp;
images of human faces was quite challenging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.740
and today, just a couple more papers down&nbsp;
the line, and we can do so much better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.120
with so much more artistic&nbsp;
control, and all this for video.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:29.400
So, let’s pop the question - how long&nbsp;
do we have to wait for such a result?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:37.200
And this is where I fell off the chair when&nbsp;
reading the paper. Don’t blink. Why? Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.900
that’s exactly how long it takes for each&nbsp;
image. We are talking high-resolution images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:49.920
and we get 5 to 10 of those&nbsp;
every second. 5 to 10! Wow.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:56.820
I would absolutely love to see what the amazing&nbsp;
artists among you Fellow Scholars will be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:02.280
to do with this. This could make the job of&nbsp;
virtual actors for animation movies easier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.200
and I bet it will be a super fun tool&nbsp;
for videoconferencing with our friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:14.880
and beloved ones, and even putting our copies&nbsp;
into virtual worlds. What a time to be alive!

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.480
Now wait, all that is well and good,&nbsp;
but when do we get to use it? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:27.720
I have two good news for you. Good news number&nbsp;
one, the source code of this project is available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:33.840
free of charge for everyone, and good news&nbsp;
number two, as of the making of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.180
you can also try it yourself online. The&nbsp;
links are available in the video description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:46.020
and make sure to read the instructions carefully.&nbsp;
Also note that the web app is a bit slower than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.460
running it locally, but of course, it is&nbsp;
so much more convenient for most people.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:56.580
Now, not even this technique is perfect, as&nbsp;
for almost all DeepFake-related techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:04.260
teeth are usually a problem. And, oh yes, sure&nbsp;
